    "intent": "pricing_analysis",
    "message": "## Direct answer\nGoverned pricing recommendation: do not make a pricing change now; keep the renewal price position unchanged pending investigation and evidence refresh. The claims view is materially weaker, with loss ratio up from 71.8% to 92.4%, and there is supporting indication of severity pressure from repair-cost inflation. However, retention and quote-to-sale conversion are broadly flat on partial-year data, competitor structured metrics show only about +1.0% average movement, and the market reports are materially conflicting, with the latest commentary indicating competitors may be cutting prices while an older report says they were increasing. Because the market evidence is both conflicting and partly stale, the available evidence does not support a governed up or down pricing movement at this time. [claims-north_west-2025-12-01] [conversion-north_west-2025-08-01] [retention-north_west-2025-08-01] [competitors-north_west-2025-12-01] [mi-conflict-repair-2025-11:chunk-001] [mi-conflict-fresh-market-2025-12:chunk-001] [mi-conflict-stale-market-2025-03:chunk-001] [pricing-history-2024-06-01] [cf-conflict-feedback-2025-12:chunk-001]\n\n## Answer by question part\n### Produce a governed pricing recommendation from the required portfolio evidence.\nRecommend an investigate / no-change action for personal motor renewals in the North West, rather than a price increase or decrease. Observed portfolio performance is weaker, especially loss ratio deterioration from 71.8% to 92.4%, and that is consistent with a severity-pressure signal from repair-cost inflation. But customer response indicators are essentially unchanged on the available 8-month window: quote-to-sale conversion stayed around 20.0% and renewal retention stayed around 85.2%. Competitor structured data shows only modest average movement of about +1.0%, while the narrative market evidence conflicts on direction and includes a stale source outside the freshness policy. That conflict prevents a supported pricing movement recommendation. The prior +1.5% action also has inconclusive tracked outcome, so it does not give reliable support for another move now. [claims-north_west-2025-12-01] [conversion-north_west-2025-08-01] [retention-north_west-2025-08-01] [competitors-north_west-2025-12-01] [mi-conflict-repair-2025-11:chunk-001] [mi-conflict-fresh-market-2025-12:chunk-001] [mi-conflict-stale-market-2025-03:chunk-001] [pricing-history-2024-06-01]\n\n## Key evidence\n- Loss ratio worsened from 71.8% to 92.4% over the analysis period, indicating materially weaker portfolio performance. [claims-north_west-2025-12-01]\n- Quote-to-sale conversion was broadly flat at about 20.0% over the observed period. [conversion-north_west-2025-08-01]\n- Renewal retention was broadly flat at about 85.2% over the observed period. [retention-north_west-2025-08-01]\n- Tracked competitors show about +1.0% average price-index movement in structured competitor analytics. [competitors-north_west-2025-12-01]\n- Repair-cost intelligence is consistent with potential severity deterioration. [mi-conflict-repair-2025-11:chunk-001]\n- Latest market commentary says competitors have cut prices in a softening market, directly conflicting with prior-quarter commentary saying competitors were raising prices. [mi-conflict-fresh-market-2025-12:chunk-001] [mi-conflict-stale-market-2025-03:chunk-001]\n- The previous +1.5% pricing action has inconclusive outcome tracking. [pricing-history-2024-06-01]\n- Customer feedback coverage is materially lower than normal, so it should carry limited weight. [cf-conflict-feedback-2025-12:chunk-001]\n\n## Interpretation\nRecommend an investigate / no-change action for personal motor renewals in the North West, rather than a price increase or decrease. Observed portfolio performance is weaker, especially loss ratio deterioration from 71.8% to 92.4%, and that is consistent with a severity-pressure signal from repair-cost inflation. But customer response indicators are essentially unchanged on the available 8-month window: quote-to-sale conversion stayed around 20.0% and renewal retention stayed around 85.2%. Competitor structured data shows only modest average movement of about +1.0%, while the narrative market evidence conflicts on direction and includes a stale source outside the freshness policy. That conflict prevents a supported pricing movement recommendation. The prior +1.5% action also has inconclusive tracked outcome, so it does not give reliable support for another move now.\n\n## Recommended action\nGoverned pricing recommendation: do not make a pricing change now; keep the renewal price position unchanged pending investigation and evidence refresh. The claims view is materially weaker, with loss ratio up from 71.8% to 92.4%, and there is supporting indication of severity pressure from repair-cost inflation. However, retention and quote-to-sale conversion are broadly flat on partial-year data, competitor structured metrics show only about +1.0% average movement, and the market reports are materially conflicting, with the latest commentary indicating competitors may be cutting prices while an older report says they were increasing. Because the market evidence is both conflicting and partly stale, the available evidence does not support a governed up or down pricing movement at this time. [claims-north_west-2025-12-01] [conversion-north_west-2025-08-01] [retention-north_west-2025-08-01] [competitors-north_west-2025-12-01] [mi-conflict-repair-2025-11:chunk-001] [mi-conflict-fresh-market-2025-12:chunk-001] [mi-conflict-stale-market-2025-03:chunk-001] [pricing-history-2024-06-01] [cf-conflict-feedback-2025-12:chunk-001]\n\n## Confidence and limitations\n- Conversion and retention evidence cover only 8 of the 12 requested monthly periods, so conclusions on customer response are based on the observed contiguous period only and may miss later-year changes.\n- One market report is outside the 120-day freshness control, reducing how much weight it can carry in the recommendation.\n- Market intelligence is materially conflicting on competitive direction, which prevents a supported recommendation for either a price increase or a price decrease.\n- The prior pricing action has inconclusive tracked outcomes, so it cannot reliably inform the next pricing move.\n- Customer feedback volume is materially lower than normal, so it does not materially strengthen the recommendation.\n\n## Specific next investigation\n- Refresh current market evidence and reconcile the conflicting market reports before considering any pricing movement.\n- Validate whether the loss ratio deterioration is primarily correlating with severity pressure versus any other portfolio mix or operational effects using refreshed actuarial review.\n- Extend conversion and retention monitoring to the missing later months so customer response evidence aligns better with the full claims period.\n- Once refreshed evidence is available, reassess whether unchanged customer metrics still hold if a pricing movement is being considered.\n- Maintain human approval control before any future pricing change, consistent with governance requirements.",
